31	% ABSTRACT
32	% ============================================================
33	\begin{abstract}
34	Benchmark contamination undermines LLM evaluation reliability, yet all existing detection methods require privileged access to model internals (token probabilities, weights, or training data). We establish the \emph{minimal-information operating point} on the contamination detection frontier by repurposing IRT person-fit statistics---developed over decades for detecting exam cheating---to operate on binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns alone. Our method, IRT-FIT, achieves AUROC~0.85 at 15\% SFT contamination and AUROC~0.93 at 25\% on controlled ground-truth experiments with Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B. We introduce a two-sided detection framework that distinguishes partial contamination (aberrant patterns, $l_z^* \ll 0$) from full contamination (over-consistent patterns, $l_z^* \gg 0$), enabling identification of Gemini-1.5's perfect MMLU accuracy ($l_z^*{=}+34.75$) as systematic contamination evidence. We derive an approximate power theory showing detection power scales as $\sqrt{n}$ with benchmark size, requiring $\geq$11.5K items for 80\% power at 15\% contamination (R$^2$=0.87 fit to simulations across 7 benchmarks). An audit of ${\sim}$250 Open LLM Leaderboard models flags 6\% as anomalous. Domain-level analysis reveals contamination is not uniform: STEM domains show systematically higher contamination prevalence than humanities. We honestly report that post-RL training attenuates detection signal (55\% retention at 25\% contamination), positioning this as an open challenge for minimal-information methods.
35	\end{abstract}
