38	% 1. INTRODUCTION
39	% ============================================================
40	\section{Introduction}
41	
42	As LLM benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow, the risk of benchmark contamination---models trained on evaluation data---threatens the reliability of the entire evaluation ecosystem \citep{sainz2023nlp,jacovi2023stop}. A contaminated LLM is functionally analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge, yet the psychometric literature on cheating detection remains unexploited in the LLM evaluation context.
43	
44	Existing contamination detection methods span five families: time-based partition \citep{golchin2024time}, membership inference / Min-K\%++ \citep{shi2024detecting}, Time Travel probing \citep{dong2024generalization}, performance-based ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat}, and in-context learning effects \citep{dong2024codec}. Critically, \emph{all} require more than binary response patterns---they need token probabilities, model weights, re-testing capability, or specially crafted prompts. \citet{wang2025post} show that all MIA-based methods fail under post-RL contamination, further motivating alternative approaches.
45	
46	We ask a foundational question: \emph{What can be detected from binary response patterns alone?} This is the minimal-information operating point on the detection frontier. Binary correct/incorrect outcomes are universally available---from leaderboard submissions, evaluation logs, and published results---making this the only access level enabling retrospective, large-scale contamination auditing without model cooperation.
47	
48	Our contributions:
49	\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
50	\item \textbf{IRT-FIT}: A two-sided detection framework using person-fit statistics ($l_z^*$) from Item Response Theory that reliably detects moderate-to-heavy SFT contamination ($\geq$15\%) from binary responses alone (AUROC 0.85 at 15\%, 0.93 at 25\%).
51	\item \textbf{Power characterization}: An approximate analytical model showing detection power $\propto \sqrt{n_{\text{items}}}$ with an inverted-U relationship to contamination level (R$^2$=0.87 across 7 benchmarks, 164--14K items).
52	\item \textbf{Empirical audit}: Application to ${\sim}$250 Open LLM Leaderboard models, flagging 6\% as anomalous, with domain-level analysis showing STEM-concentrated contamination patterns.
53	\end{enumerate}
54	
