56	% 2. RELATED WORK
57	% ============================================================
58	\section{Related Work}
59	
60	\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}
61	Contamination detection methods can be organized along an \emph{information access frontier}. At the highest access level, methods requiring training data use n-gram overlap \citep{dodge2021documenting}. Gray-box methods exploit token probabilities: Min-K\% \citep{shi2024detecting} and Min-K\%++ use minimum token log-probabilities as membership signals. Time Travel \citep{dong2024generalization} uses model-generated completions of benchmark prefixes. ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat} requires re-testing on rephrased items. CoDeC \citep{dong2024codec} leverages in-context learning dynamics. \citet{wang2025post} demonstrate that post-RL training breaks MIA-based detectors. No existing method operates at the binary-only level that IRT-FIT targets.
62	
63	\paragraph{IRT for LLMs.}
64	Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation for ability estimation \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}, benchmark design \citep{rodriguez2021evaluation}, and the PSN-IRT framework \citep{gu2024psn} calibrating 4PL models across 12 LLMs on 5 benchmarks. We extend this line by applying \emph{person-fit} statistics---the psychometric toolkit for detecting examinees with preknowledge---to contamination detection.
65	
66	\paragraph{Person-Fit Statistics.}
67	Person-fit indices identify response patterns inconsistent with expected IRT behavior \citep{karabatsos2003comparing}. The standardized $l_z^*$ statistic \citep{snijders2001item} has known asymptotic properties under correct model specification. \citet{hong2021multiscale} extend to multiscale aggregation across subtests. To our knowledge, no prior work applies person-fit statistics to LLM benchmark contamination.
