70	% 3. METHOD
71	% ============================================================
72	\section{Method}
73	
74	\subsection{IRT Background: The 4PL Model}
75	
76	Item Response Theory models the probability that examinee $j$ with ability $\theta_j$ correctly answers item $i$ with parameters $(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)$:
77	\begin{equation}
78	P(X_{ij}=1|\theta_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-a_i(\theta_j - b_i))}
79	\label{eq:4pl}
80	\end{equation}
81	where $a_i$ is discrimination, $b_i$ is difficulty, $c_i$ is the guessing parameter (lower asymptote), and $d_i$ is the feasibility parameter (upper asymptote, $d_i \leq 1$). When $d_i = 1$, this reduces to the 3PL model. The 4PL's upper asymptote is critical for LLM evaluation: even the highest-ability model may not achieve perfect accuracy on every item due to ambiguity or annotation noise, so $d_i < 1$ items exist where perfect performance is genuinely surprising.
82	
83	We use 4PL item parameters calibrated by the PSN-IRT framework \citep{gu2024psn} across 5 benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K, HumanEval) using responses from 12 LLMs.
84	
85	\subsection{Person-Fit Statistics: The $l_z^*$ Index}
86	
87	Given a response vector $\mathbf{x}_j = (x_{j1}, \ldots, x_{jn})$ and estimated ability $\hat{\theta}_j$, the log-likelihood person-fit statistic is:
88	\begin{equation}
89	l_0(\mathbf{x}_j) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left[ x_{ji} \log P_i(\hat{\theta}_j) + (1-x_{ji}) \log(1 - P_i(\hat{\theta}_j)) \right]
90	\end{equation}
91	The standardized form \citep{snijders2001item} corrects for ability estimation:
92	\begin{equation}
93	l_z^* = \frac{l_0 - E(l_0 | \hat{\theta}_j)}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(l_0 | \hat{\theta}_j)}}
94	\label{eq:lzstar}
95	\end{equation}
96	Under the null hypothesis (no contamination), $l_z^* \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ asymptotically. A contaminated examinee---one who has seen benchmark items during training---produces response patterns deviating from the IRT model's predictions.
97	
98	\subsection{Two-Sided Detection Framework}
99	\label{sec:twosided}
100	
101	A key insight is that contamination produces \emph{two distinct} signal regimes depending on contamination severity:
102	
103	\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
104	\item \textbf{Partial contamination} (${\sim}$5--50\% items): The model memorizes a subset of items, answering them correctly regardless of difficulty. This creates \emph{aberrant} patterns where easy items may be wrong while hard contaminated items are correct, yielding $l_z^* \ll 0$.
105	\item \textbf{Full contamination} (${\sim}$75--100\% items): The model achieves near-perfect accuracy. With 4PL items where $d_i < 1$, even maximum-ability examinees are expected to err occasionally. Perfect accuracy is thus \emph{over-consistent}, yielding $l_z^* \gg 0$.
106	\end{itemize}
107	
108	We apply a two-sided test at $\alpha = 0.05$:
109	\begin{equation}
110	\text{Flag} = \begin{cases}
111	\text{aberrant (partial contamination)} & \text{if } l_z^* < -1.96 \\
112	\text{over-consistent (full contamination)} & \text{if } l_z^* > +1.96 \\
113	\text{non-significant} & \text{otherwise}
114	\end{cases}
115	\end{equation}
116	with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction when testing multiple models simultaneously.
117	
118	\subsection{Multiscale Aggregation ($l_z^m$)}
119	
120	Following \citet{hong2021multiscale}, we aggregate person-fit signals across $K$ benchmarks using the multiscale statistic:
121	\begin{equation}
122	l_z^m = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{K} l_{z,k}^*}{\sqrt{K + 2\sum_{k<k'} \hat{\rho}_{kk'}}}
123	\end{equation}
124	where $\hat{\rho}_{kk'}$ is the estimated correlation between $l_z^*$ values across benchmarks. Under the null, $l_z^m \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$. This aggregation increases effective sample size and reduces false positives when contamination spans multiple benchmarks.
125	
126	\subsection{Power Theory}
127	\label{sec:power}
128	
129	We derive an approximate relationship between detection power, benchmark size $n$, and contamination level $c$. Under contamination level $c$, the expected $l_z^*$ shift is $\delta(c)$, and detection power is:
130	\begin{equation}
131	P(\text{detect} \mid n, c) \approx \Phi\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{n} \cdot \delta(c)}{\sigma_{l_z^*}(n)} - z_\alpha \right)
132	\label{eq:power}
133	\end{equation}
134	where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF and $\sigma_{l_z^*}(n)$ follows from Snijders' variance correction $\text{Var}(l_z^*) = 1 + O(1/n)$.
135	
136	The effect size $\delta(c)$ is non-monotonic in $c$: as contamination approaches 100\%, ability estimation $\hat{\theta} \to \infty$, and under the 4PL model the expected response pattern converges to $(d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_n)$---the upper asymptotes. If the model also answers all items correctly, the surprise per item vanishes for items with $d_i \approx 1$, reducing $|\delta(c)|$. This produces an \emph{inverted-U}: power first rises with $c$ (more contaminated items $\to$ stronger aberrant signal) then falls (ability inflation $\to$ expected perfect performance $\to$ weaker surprise). The inflection occurs at $c \approx 0.50$--0.60 depending on the benchmark's item parameter distribution (Section~\ref{sec:simulation}).
137	
138	\paragraph{Practical implication.} From Eq.~\ref{eq:power}, the minimum benchmark size for 80\% power at contamination level $c$ can be estimated. At $c=0.15$: $n \geq 11{,}500$ items; at $c=0.25$: $n \geq 4{,}200$ items. These thresholds, while approximate (R$^2$=0.87 fit to simulation data), provide actionable guidance for benchmark design.
