462	% ============================================================
463	\section{Conclusion}
464	
465	We introduced IRT-FIT, a contamination detection method operating at the minimal-information point of the detection frontier---requiring only binary response patterns. Through controlled experiments on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B, we demonstrated reliable detection of moderate-to-heavy SFT contamination ($\geq$15\%, AUROC 0.85--0.93). The two-sided detection framework distinguishes partial from full contamination, and domain-level analysis localizes contamination to specific knowledge areas (STEM $>$ Humanities). An approximate power theory (R$^2$=0.87) provides actionable guidance: benchmarks need $\geq$11.5K items for 80\% power at 15\% contamination.
466	
467	\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Post-RL training attenuates detection signal by ${\sim}$45\%, rendering $<$20\% contamination undetectable after RL---a fundamental limitation of binary-only information. (2) The leaderboard audit covers only ${\sim}$250 models (not the full 7K+ available). (3) Controlled experiments use 2 model families; broader validation is needed. (4) Systematic positive bias in $l_z^*$ across LLMs suggests potential calibration mismatch between IRT item parameters and the LLM population.
468	
469	\paragraph{Future work.} Post-RL contamination detection via hybrid binary+behavioral signals; recalibration of IRT parameters on LLM-specific populations; extension to generation-based benchmarks beyond multiple-choice.
